<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=smartphone">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Smartphone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A smartphone is a mobile device that combines a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mobile-phone">mobile phone</a> with advanced computing, typically a touchscreen, web browsing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/email">email</a>, a camera and downloadable applications. The first commercial smartphones are widely recognised to be the IBM Simon Personal Communicator, released in 1992, and General Magic's Magic Cap devices, sold by Sony and Motorola in 1994. After the iPhone became popular in the late 2000s, most smartphones took a slate form with a large capacitive multi-touch screen. Apple's App Store opened in July 2008, and in 2019 alone 1.54 billion units were shipped worldwide.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/smartphone.jpg" alt="Smartphone" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sage Ross · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wikipedia_homepage_on_a_large_Android_phone,_2015-04-16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
